WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Translator: Louise-Marie Six
Reviewer: Denise RQ

00:00:10.289 --> 00:00:12.099
Could you please pick the odd one out?

00:00:14.807 --> 00:00:16.599
It's not difficult, right?

00:00:17.159 --> 00:00:22.997
The image you see on the screen
represents, some think, a rough idea

00:00:22.998 --> 00:00:27.998
of what the British and general
film-making industry looks like.

00:00:27.999 --> 00:00:32.052
Figures revealed by the Women's
Media Center in New York

00:00:32.054 --> 00:00:36.928
show that across a five-year period
ending in the year 2012,

00:00:36.929 --> 00:00:39.858
of the 500 top-grossing movies,

00:00:39.859 --> 00:00:45.011
only two of those had
Black women directors attached to them.

00:00:45.012 --> 00:00:48.659
That's 0.4%.

00:00:49.601 --> 00:00:53.150
I'm thinking back now

00:00:53.151 --> 00:00:56.209
to the night
before I was about to set foot

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.234
on the set of my first movie
ever to direct.

00:00:59.235 --> 00:01:03.102
And I can tell you now:
I was absolutely terrified.

00:01:03.103 --> 00:01:06.631
My fear pivoted
around the fact that I knew

00:01:06.632 --> 00:01:10.192
that I wasn't what was
expected of a director.

00:01:10.193 --> 00:01:12.952
I didn't fit
the industry model, full stop.

00:01:13.169 --> 00:01:16.143
I wondered how I was going to lead

00:01:16.144 --> 00:01:19.619
my all-male crew,
and they were also all white,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:23.419
how I was going to instill
confidence in them

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.158
and get them to believe in me,

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:30.451
so that I could end up with a film
that I had written on screen.

00:01:30.452 --> 00:01:36.749
After all, I knew that they had never
worked under anyone like me before;

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.798
my shape, my flavor.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:42.170
(Laughter.)

00:01:42.171 --> 00:01:45.401
I realized, essentially,

00:01:45.402 --> 00:01:50.520
that I knew
the very world that I wanted to work in

00:01:50.521 --> 00:01:55.240
as one that, statistically,
did not include me.

00:01:55.241 --> 00:02:00.570
And that society,
by its own boundaries and perceptions,

00:02:00.571 --> 00:02:05.016
had created me to be somebody
that didn't fit into the category

00:02:05.017 --> 00:02:09.551
of what film-makers were
generally expected to be.

00:02:09.553 --> 00:02:12.934
And yet, I felt like a director.

00:02:12.935 --> 00:02:17.272
I knew that I could craft stories onscreen

00:02:17.273 --> 00:02:20.352
and connect those stories to audiences.

00:02:20.353 --> 00:02:23.352
But the figures don't lie,

00:02:23.353 --> 00:02:26.633
and the statistics
did not tally in my favor.

00:02:26.853 --> 00:02:32.318
Essentially, my definition of me
didn't match the definition

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:35.674
that society had bestowed upon me.

00:02:35.935 --> 00:02:37.972
And so, who wins?

00:02:38.454 --> 00:02:41.957
That's what I want
to talk to you about, today.

00:02:42.121 --> 00:02:47.574
The question of who defines you:
society or yourself?

00:02:48.831 --> 00:02:54.872
Is society's opinion
of who you are, who you can be,

00:02:54.873 --> 00:02:58.054
what value you have,
what you can achieve in life,

00:02:58.055 --> 00:03:03.795
is that more powerful an opinion
and important an opinion than your own?

00:03:04.441 --> 00:03:06.775
If you say you are a writer,

00:03:06.776 --> 00:03:11.796
and you have something valuable
to contribute to the literary world?

00:03:11.797 --> 00:03:15.848
But reviewers and critic say you're not,

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:17.565
you're not a great writer.

00:03:17.566 --> 00:03:20.237
Then whose truth wins?

00:03:20.238 --> 00:03:25.876
And whose truth is most likely
to determine your future as a writer?

00:03:26.295 --> 00:03:29.767
For me, the answer is really simple.

00:03:29.768 --> 00:03:32.518
It's whichever you choose to believe.

00:03:33.375 --> 00:03:36.249
I have this idea

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:40.779
that since we can all dream
a far bigger dream for ourselves

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:44.666
than society could ever
indicate for any of us,

00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:51.086
what would happen if we all decided
we were going to actively define ourselves

00:03:51.426 --> 00:03:55.056
and allow that definition
to govern everything,

00:03:55.057 --> 00:03:59.017
over and above anything that society
could define or indicate for any of us?

00:03:59.018 --> 00:04:02.927
How would that influence our lives?

00:04:03.684 --> 00:04:07.623
The question of who defines us

00:04:07.624 --> 00:04:11.430
comes up in everybody's life
at some point or another.

00:04:11.431 --> 00:04:16.144
It doesn't have to be
about race or gender, necessarily.

00:04:16.144 --> 00:04:19.882
For me, the question
embedded itself long ago.

00:04:20.043 --> 00:04:21.829
I grew up down the road from here,

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:27.640
at a time when Brixton was being
so negatively defined by society,

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:32.416
and also defined in a way
that it didn't authentically see itself,

00:04:32.417 --> 00:04:37.939
that it practically imploded on itself,
in its struggle to be heard.

00:04:38.258 --> 00:04:41.904
With an older brother,
at the time, who was still a teenager,

00:04:41.905 --> 00:04:45.406
I experienced how it felt for some

00:04:45.431 --> 00:04:49.239
that they were living in a police state.

00:04:49.813 --> 00:04:51.754
But yet, I also witnessed

00:04:51.755 --> 00:04:57.295
how Brixton refused to be
marginalized and criminalized,

00:04:57.296 --> 00:05:02.115
as it rose up
during the riots of the 1980s

00:05:02.116 --> 00:05:06.092
and rejected this definition

00:05:06.117 --> 00:05:10.776
of the existence it should have

00:05:10.777 --> 00:05:13.386
by society's standards.

00:05:14.566 --> 00:05:19.742
Defining yourself
outside of the realms of society,

00:05:19.743 --> 00:05:21.107
it's not easy.

00:05:21.637 --> 00:05:23.656
As a writer and director,

00:05:23.657 --> 00:05:27.529
it's such an interesting
and fascinating subject to me

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:28.911
that it has risen to the fore

00:05:28.912 --> 00:05:31.086
of every single project
I have ever worked on,

00:05:31.087 --> 00:05:33.637
and probably every
single project I will ever do.

00:05:33.847 --> 00:05:38.076
I think the reason is
because it's so complex.

00:05:38.077 --> 00:05:40.828
I think what's so hard about it

00:05:40.829 --> 00:05:43.897
is to recognize when your choices
and your direction in life

00:05:43.898 --> 00:05:50.710
are being influenced by society's
perhaps more restricted ideas

00:05:50.711 --> 00:05:52.392
of who you should be.

00:05:52.393 --> 00:05:57.337
When the mirror image
of what society is suggesting to you

00:05:57.338 --> 00:06:00.519
doesn't reflect the person
that you really feel you are.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:05.543
Or even worse, when the untrue image
that's coming back to you

00:06:05.544 --> 00:06:07.848
does feel real.

00:06:07.851 --> 00:06:12.708
Because the messages
and the influence can be so subtle

00:06:12.709 --> 00:06:15.089
that it's just easy to dismiss.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.571
"This is just the way
things are supposed to be,"

00:06:17.572 --> 00:06:19.419
that's what you tell yourself.

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:23.368
You think about
how many used that argument

00:06:23.369 --> 00:06:26.696
when it came
to the transatlantic slave trade,

00:06:26.697 --> 00:06:29.512
or against the women
who demanded the vote,

00:06:29.513 --> 00:06:32.674
or even today, when it comes
to gay marriage.

00:06:33.165 --> 00:06:36.882
Think of how many institutions
I can think of them right now...

00:06:36.883 --> 00:06:42.010
And organizations use
outdated, antiquated, biased rules

00:06:42.011 --> 00:06:46.505
to maintain the status quo;
and sometimes, an unjust status quo.

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:50.331
We have been doing this
for x and x amount of years,

00:06:50.332 --> 00:06:52.102
so we can't change it now,

00:06:52.103 --> 00:06:54.316
it's just... it's tradition.

00:06:54.317 --> 00:06:56.692
It's a word they like to use: 'tradition.'

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:03.019
For me, I think that it sounds uninvolved

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:06.793
to not move forward
because you think something is tradition.

00:07:06.794 --> 00:07:10.592
And yet, when society is acting

00:07:10.593 --> 00:07:14.433
to prevent you from reaching
your goals and your dreams,

00:07:14.434 --> 00:07:19.953
I think that's precisely the time
you have to truly remember who you are.

00:07:20.763 --> 00:07:25.103
The reason why I believe that
is because today,

00:07:25.104 --> 00:07:28.062
none of use emerge
from just one single world.

00:07:28.063 --> 00:07:31.441
We are all a combination
of many worlds put together.

00:07:31.442 --> 00:07:34.813
Sometimes, when you are
at the junction of many worlds,

00:07:34.814 --> 00:07:38.355
society considers you a contradiction.

00:07:39.244 --> 00:07:42.087
Growing up, as I said,
down the road from here,

00:07:42.088 --> 00:07:46.698
I experienced the common story
of constantly being asked,

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:49.246
"Where are you from? Where are you from?"

00:07:49.247 --> 00:07:52.616
And I would say, "Well, actually,
I was born here in London."

00:07:52.617 --> 00:07:54.457
And then I would be harangued.

00:07:54.458 --> 00:07:56.607
"No, but where are you really from?"

00:07:56.608 --> 00:07:59.938
"What part of 'London' don't you get?"

00:07:59.939 --> 00:08:04.485
And then interestingly,
I would go back to Ghana

00:08:04.486 --> 00:08:09.942
to visit both my grandmothers,
with my mother during summer vacation,

00:08:09.943 --> 00:08:12.984
and I would be told, "Well,
you're not really Ghanaian, are you?

00:08:12.985 --> 00:08:14.911
Because you're too English."

00:08:14.912 --> 00:08:15.941
It got to the point

00:08:15.942 --> 00:08:18.328
where at this junction
that I was sitting at,

00:08:18.329 --> 00:08:21.484
I didn't know which pathway to go down.

00:08:21.485 --> 00:08:23.988
Because, if I took one pathway,
it would be rejected,

00:08:23.989 --> 00:08:26.837
if I took another pathway,
that would be rejected.

00:08:26.838 --> 00:08:33.285
It felt like, in many ways,
society was defining me as an outsider.

00:08:34.482 --> 00:08:37.590
As I say, again, and I'll keep saying it,

00:08:37.591 --> 00:08:41.241
this doesn't have to be
solely about race or gender.

00:08:41.241 --> 00:08:43.289
We're all at the junction of many worlds.

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:46.779
It can be concerning race,

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:50.650
it can be concerning class, sexuality;

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:54.578
so many different things, today.

00:08:54.579 --> 00:08:56.768
I don't think...
I am wondering, at least,

00:08:56.769 --> 00:09:00.830
if there is a person in this room
who, at least at some point in their life,

00:09:00.831 --> 00:09:04.253
hasn't been identified
outside of the realms

00:09:04.254 --> 00:09:07.998
of that which they have felt
that they would see themselves,

00:09:07.999 --> 00:09:12.173
or at some point at least,
hasn't felt like an outsider.

00:09:13.372 --> 00:09:18.211
For me, I don't think it should be about
having to pick one pathway over another.

00:09:18.212 --> 00:09:22.205
I shouldn't have to pick
being British over being Ghanaian,

00:09:22.206 --> 00:09:24.670
or being Ghanaian over being British,

00:09:24.671 --> 00:09:28.901
because ultimately,
I am a product of both.

00:09:28.909 --> 00:09:32.096
Both of those cultures reside inside me.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:38.541
It shouldn't really matter
if that's not normal to society.

00:09:38.983 --> 00:09:43.766
For me, it's not so much
about a collision of worlds

00:09:43.767 --> 00:09:46.931
as it is a combining of worlds.

00:09:47.272 --> 00:09:49.921
As I say, it really shouldn't matter

00:09:49.922 --> 00:09:52.582
whether society
defines it as normal or not,

00:09:52.583 --> 00:09:59.042
because as the late
Dr. Maya Angelou would say,

00:09:59.043 --> 00:10:01.931
"If we are always trying
so hard to be normal,

00:10:01.932 --> 00:10:06.337
how will we ever know
how amazing you can be."

00:10:07.147 --> 00:10:14.073
Bringing to bear
your definition of yourself in society,

00:10:14.074 --> 00:10:18.495
- as I say, I know it can be hard -
there are all sorts of things:

00:10:18.496 --> 00:10:23.103
beauty ideals, gender expectations
for both men and women,

00:10:23.104 --> 00:10:26.321
racial profiling, class prejudice;

00:10:26.322 --> 00:10:31.002
these are all examples
of how we can be thrown off base

00:10:31.003 --> 00:10:37.895
in terms of knowing, and understanding,
and defining who we know we truly are.

00:10:39.285 --> 00:10:41.186
I am thinking now

00:10:44.086 --> 00:10:47.173
about the guy who wants
to stand in his truth

00:10:47.174 --> 00:10:49.403
when it comes to his sexuality,

00:10:49.404 --> 00:10:55.219
even though his community or his religion
might expect something different of him.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:59.646
Those who find
they define themselves as beautiful,

00:10:59.647 --> 00:11:03.215
but society is telling them
that they are not.

00:11:03.216 --> 00:11:08.483
I think to myself that at those points

00:11:08.484 --> 00:11:13.416
exactly when society is telling you
you are not who you feel you are,

00:11:13.417 --> 00:11:14.948
that's the time, as I say,

00:11:14.949 --> 00:11:20.055
you have to understand the power
of what it means to define yourself.

00:11:20.056 --> 00:11:21.903
How it can mean the difference

00:11:21.904 --> 00:11:26.205
between whether you may be happy
or less happy in life,

00:11:26.206 --> 00:11:28.981
a failure or a success in life,

00:11:28.982 --> 00:11:30.805
but most importantly,

00:11:30.806 --> 00:11:35.926
conscious or unconscious
in the story of your own life.

00:11:36.562 --> 00:11:43.506
I think back now to finding my way
originally in the business of film,

00:11:43.507 --> 00:11:48.525
and being told so often it's very hard

00:11:48.526 --> 00:11:52.387
to sell Black movies
with a Black protagonist.

00:11:52.388 --> 00:11:55.477
There are enough movies today

00:11:55.478 --> 00:12:00.009
that have defied that definition
and shown that to be untrue.

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:05.321
But regardless of whether that was just
opinion, or whether that was perception,

00:12:05.322 --> 00:12:07.209
or whether it was truth or not,

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:10.607
I realized that if I was going
to move forward in the business,

00:12:10.608 --> 00:12:17.158
I was going to have to try and find a way
of working out what the obstacles were

00:12:17.159 --> 00:12:20.849
and finding a way
to negotiate those obstacles.

00:12:21.266 --> 00:12:22.698
What I learned is

00:12:22.699 --> 00:12:27.366
that when you truly embrace who you are,

00:12:27.367 --> 00:12:30.366
and all of the worlds that you come from,

00:12:30.367 --> 00:12:33.946
when you bring those sensibilities

00:12:33.971 --> 00:12:37.629
to a previously closed off world,

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:40.851
you move society forward.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:46.999
Because when we move into other worlds,
we touch people, we impact people.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:51.180
And when people change,
then society has to, as well.

00:12:51.181 --> 00:12:56.795
For me, the value of embracing

00:12:56.796 --> 00:12:59.615
all of the worlds that exist
in you, and get this,

00:12:59.616 --> 00:13:04.101
is that those of us who are able
to do that have an insight

00:13:04.102 --> 00:13:10.010
that shows us why colliding worlds
on the outside are not a bad thing.

00:13:10.011 --> 00:13:14.741
Because when worlds meet,
progress can follow.

00:13:14.742 --> 00:13:20.023
The mother who may run
the Fortune 500 company,

00:13:20.024 --> 00:13:24.025
the son of a cleaner who becomes a doctor,

00:13:24.026 --> 00:13:27.197
or think about the first
African-American children

00:13:27.198 --> 00:13:32.384
to set foot in the all-white schools
after segregation was abolished;

00:13:32.385 --> 00:13:38.205
those are all people
that inspire me to do what I do.

00:13:38.665 --> 00:13:45.195
I'm thinking now about the women
who do make their way

00:13:45.196 --> 00:13:48.606
in the male-dominated world
of film-making.

00:13:48.607 --> 00:13:50.555
What we realize about those women

00:13:50.556 --> 00:13:53.073
is that when they are able
to break into that world,

00:13:53.074 --> 00:13:55.383
they make an impact.

00:13:55.384 --> 00:14:01.728
The British Film Institute
released figures between 2010 and 2012

00:14:01.729 --> 00:14:06.618
that show, that although women
were still underrepresented

00:14:06.619 --> 00:14:09.838
when it came to independent film-making,

00:14:09.839 --> 00:14:14.246
they represented
a significant figure or percentage

00:14:14.247 --> 00:14:16.757
when it came to successful films.

00:14:16.758 --> 00:14:18.989
People love their films.

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:22.353
That's what I mean when I say
when we have the courage

00:14:22.354 --> 00:14:26.580
to move forward into other worlds,
we have the impact to change them.

00:14:26.809 --> 00:14:31.970
It takes me back to the story
that I started with,

00:14:31.971 --> 00:14:34.020
stepping onto the set of my first film.

00:14:34.021 --> 00:14:39.253
A few weeks before I was
about to shoot that movie,

00:14:39.713 --> 00:14:42.877
a film executive came up to me and said,

00:14:42.878 --> 00:14:47.438
"Well, you're not going to step on set
with your big high heels on, are you?"

00:14:47.456 --> 00:14:51.630
As if to say me coming on set
with high heels was really going to be

00:14:51.631 --> 00:14:55.410
the thing that established me
as a woman a man's world.

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:59.050
For the record,
I don't direct in high heels,

00:14:59.051 --> 00:15:01.308
and I didn't direct
my first film in high heels,

00:15:01.309 --> 00:15:03.351
except from the last day.

00:15:03.369 --> 00:15:05.130
I always make that a tradition.

00:15:05.131 --> 00:15:09.206
But I did celebrate
my love for heels in this way.

00:15:09.207 --> 00:15:11.632
I don't know how clear
that is for everybody,

00:15:11.633 --> 00:15:16.902
but there is essentially a high heel
on the name of my company.

00:15:16.903 --> 00:15:20.827
That appeared at the end of the movie,
and it was the logo for my company.

00:15:20.828 --> 00:15:26.601
I believe in leaning in to our identity,

00:15:26.602 --> 00:15:28.769
not leaning back.

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:34.236
High heels are definitely
a part of my identity.

00:15:34.968 --> 00:15:38.755
But you see, I believe
the part of me that loves high heels

00:15:38.756 --> 00:15:43.241
and loves being a woman
is every bit the part of me

00:15:43.242 --> 00:15:46.577
that is able to write movies,

00:15:46.602 --> 00:15:50.715
direct movies, and deliver movies.

00:15:50.716 --> 00:15:56.610
Just as in the way that the part of me
where Africa meets Europe

00:15:56.611 --> 00:16:01.373
is the thing that gives me
my unique, yet universal eye.

00:16:02.061 --> 00:16:06.704
I believe every industry,
every business, every society

00:16:06.705 --> 00:16:10.480
requires fresh eyes to open new windows

00:16:10.505 --> 00:16:13.963
on old concepts and unseen worlds,

00:16:13.964 --> 00:16:18.410
to bring new perspectives
to the universal experience.

00:16:19.561 --> 00:16:22.061
The journey for me of defining myself

00:16:22.062 --> 00:16:27.651
is an ongoing experience;
I work at it every day.

00:16:27.652 --> 00:16:31.340
But if you ask me the question today,
"Who are you Amma? Where are you from?"

00:16:31.341 --> 00:16:34.954
I can tell you this:
I am a Black British woman.

00:16:34.955 --> 00:16:37.903
I am proudly born of Ghanaian parents.

00:16:37.904 --> 00:16:41.457
I am a screenwriter and a movie director.

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:43.868
How will you define you?

00:16:44.284 --> 00:16:45.403
Thank you.

00:16:45.404 --> 00:16:46.800
(Applause)

